  {
    "episode_id": 72741,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72741.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 3,
    "turn_text": "Well, I think in any legislation like this, you will have a certain title in the Farm Bill and a certain part of that title that a member feels very strongly about. Obviously, the nutrition title was one of those. And I understand that. I respect that. But in the end result, to be quite frank, you have to count votes. You can be interested in policy and insist upon that and have an issue and then not have a farm bill. And then that really strikes at all the rest of the farm bill that is a very good safety net for everybody concerned. So compromise is not a four-letter word with regards to what we do in Washington today. The vast majority of people I talk to say, why can't you get along and get something done? Well, we did that. It took us two years. It's a pretty easy task if you listen to farmers and then go ahead and try to produce what in fact they actually need. So the key was bipartisanship and the key was to get everybody to understand we were short on time and we had to get this thing done.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
