  {
    "episode_id": 72741,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72741.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 5,
    "turn_text": "Well, again, look at the situation we're in. In 1996, I was part of a very revolutionary farm bill called Freedom to Farm. somewhat controversial to say the least, but we changed this business farmers planning according to Washington as opposed to the market. That was key, but it was a tough sell. We didn't get it the first year. We got it in the next few months. This time we can't do that. We're in a very rough patch. So this is an evolutionary bill that is very predictable and it will enable our farmers and ranchers and and growers to go to their lenders and say, I'm good for the next five years. So it was a different circumstance with regard to payment limitations. There are a lot of folks in trouble, not only small farmers, not only small farming operations or specialty crops or whatever, but you have especially down south and out west where people are involved in lots of acres and a lot of expense. That whole operation may be very big to some people, But the other thing is that the Vietnam veterans who are farmers, they're getting up in years and they'd like to leave the farm if possible to that son or daughter. But maybe they don't want to do that. Maybe the son and daughter is gone. So we were worried about that. So now we have a cousin that maybe has been working the farm or a niece or a nephew. I think that's perfectly understandable in the dramatic situation that we find ourselves in with You've got to have access to technology and everything else. That's very costly. So a big operation can fail just as well as the small ones. So the payment limit reform that we did put in tried to address that issue.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
