    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 72741,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72741.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 7,
    "turn_text": "Well, we had a situation with a cushion of credit where we tried to inform and signal to those folks that we were talking about 5% on a rainy day fund. And when, you know, interest rates are far below that, just the public outcry alone would have been We don't need to do anything to hinder these folks who provide such a valuable service. So we work with them. I'm sure that they would prefer it the other way. But I think what happened over a period of years is very understandable and they can work with that. As to the other programs, we tried to consolidate them. We, I think, reformed that whole situation by combining programs. You do that, you get savings. And by the way, I want to thank the CBO folks back here. Sometimes I'm very critical of those folks, but they were very good to work with, given the fact we were under a deadline. I also want to give a shout out to the USDA folks that when we got to Real question on what a change would actually mean out in the countryside. We tried to work with our land grad schools and obviously our state departments of agriculture. But the USDA came in and gave us the best information. And I thank Sonny Perdue for enabling us to have that partnership.",
