  },
  {
    "episode_id": 72741,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72741.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 15,
    "turn_text": "Yes, it did. Credit Chairman Conway for his effort. I was concerned that there is a tangible benefit with regards to acres that have not been tilled over a period of years and that the lender, if the fall of a sudden the farmer awakened to the fact after the farm bills passed and the FSA folks said, oh, by the way, those 10% of your ground is no longer in your acreage base. The person that would take real note of that would be the lender. And I don't know when the farmer goes in and talks to somebody, and you've seen a 10% loss in your acreage base, what happened? We tried to work with the USDA to predict exactly what happened, who would be hurt, where that would happen, most of it's in Texas, and a lot of it in Oklahoma, and a great deal of it in Kansas. So you had to figure out how many people would be harmed by this, as well as the trade-off of getting at least a CBO estimate on cost savings. I think we worked it out. I think we worked it out well to the best final product that we could.",
