  {
    "episode_id": 72741,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72741.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 23,
    "turn_text": "I think Zippy hit the nail on the head. I want to thank the Farm Bureau. And for that matter, every other commodity organization and farm organization, we have over 1,000 different organizations that have come in and supported this bill. That's never happened before. Of course, we never got 87 votes either before. So that's set a record. But given that, and given everybody's worry about Climate change, which could be a factor, probably is, with regards to hurricanes. And if you look at the Great Plains, you know, look down the road for 10 years, we had a purifier here two years ago. Over 800,000 acres didn't get much press in southwest Kansas, but it was devastating. If we didn't have the EQUIP program, I don't know what we would have done. To get that kind of disaster assistance, and that's what you're talking about, I'm not sure the farm program can do that. The farm program can give a safety net for producers, but with regards to a national disaster, which is what we're facing usually with a hurricane, or for that matter, a bad season of tornadoes, et cetera, et cetera. That also goes to other committees and jurisdiction, but I think Zippy hit the nail on the head, And that is if we have experienced this for the last four or five years, I hasten to add that we had the dirty 30s and then we were dry in the 50s and then we were dry again in the 60s. So these things happen. But these seem to happen more often today and they're very damaging without question. But we will work with other committees to try to come up with an answer to that. It's going to be costly. and that's going to have to be shared with states and with individuals concerned.",
