  {
    "episode_id": 72741,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72741.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 25,
    "turn_text": "No, I don't. I've heard that song. It gets to be number one or two or three on the country hit list. This is the last Farm Bill. I'm not going to sing it. It's always difficult. It took us two years to go to almost every state in the Union, every region. And we did that. Senator Stabenot and I have a relationship where I certainly respect her. I trust her. She's a friend. And just as important, the same thing goes for our staffs. We don't agree on a lot of things, but we agree to get things done. And I trust her. In this atmosphere we have in Washington, that's unique. We're getting a lot of questions is how come, you know, how do you get along with every Savinol with just five? We did this before in 212, approaching the 214 Bell, but unfortunately that hit a brick wall over there in the house. I think if you have a relationship with your minority partner and you work hard to get a bipartisan bill, And you count the votes and you have enough to get 60 in the Senate. And then you work with the House just as best as you can and know that they're a partnership as well. I think you can get a Farm Bill. And we did. A lot of people said that this would be the last, or the previous Farm Bill would be the last Farm Bill. It's too important to the country. I mean, this is a, this is a national bill, over $800 billion and then some and 12 titles that affects everybody in this country. So, I don't buy that that this is the last farm bill.",
